    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Before building on new SS3 content, it is useful to recall the core citizenship concepts studied in SS1 and SS2, since SS3 topics build directly on this foundation.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Key Concepts Recalled</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Citizenship</td><td>Membership of a state, carrying both rights and duties</td></tr><tr><td>Citizen</td><td>A legally recognised member of a state, entitled to its protection and privileges</td></tr><tr><td>Nationality</td><td>Legal belonging to a nation, often by birth, descent or naturalisation</td></tr><tr><td>Rights</td><td>Entitlements guaranteed to citizens, such as the right to life, freedom of speech and freedom of movement</td></tr><tr><td>Duties/Obligations</td><td>Responsibilities a citizen owes the state, such as obeying laws and paying taxes</td></tr><tr><td>Good governance</td><td>Government conducted with transparency, accountability, participation and the rule of law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Ways of Acquiring Citizenship</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>By birth:</strong> being born within the territory of a country, or to parents who are citizens.</li><li><strong>By descent:</strong> through one or both parents' nationality.</li><li><strong>By registration:</strong> for certain qualified persons, such as those married to citizens.</li><li><strong>By naturalisation:</strong> a formal legal process for a foreigner who meets residency and other requirements to become a citizen.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Rights and Duties of a Citizen</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Rights (examples)</th><th>Duties (examples)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Right to life</td><td>Obey the laws of the land</td></tr><tr><td>Right to freedom of expression</td><td>Pay taxes</td></tr><tr><td>Right to vote and be voted for</td><td>Respect the rights of others</td></tr><tr><td>Right to own property</td><td>Protect public property</td></tr><tr><td>Right to freedom of movement</td><td>Participate in nation building</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Why This Review Matters</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">SS3 Citizenship and Heritage Studies moves into more complex themes — national identity, global citizenship, human rights, and civic competence — all of which depend on a firm grasp of the basic concepts of citizenship, rights and duties covered earlier.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to distinguish clearly between the different ways of acquiring citizenship — birth, descent, registration and naturalisation.</div>",
