    "module": "Heritage and National Development",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Heritage</span> refers to the traditions, culture, monuments, values and practices passed down from past generations. Understanding how heritage shapes life today is central to this topic.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Heritage</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Heritage is the collective inheritance of cultural, historical and natural assets — including customs, languages, art, architecture, festivals and values — that a society receives from its past and preserves for future generations.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Heritage</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tangible heritage</td><td>Monuments, historical buildings, artefacts, archaeological sites (e.g. Sungbo's Eredo, Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove)</td></tr><tr><td>Intangible heritage</td><td>Festivals, oral traditions, music, dance, language, indigenous knowledge</td></tr><tr><td>Natural heritage</td><td>National parks, forests, and natural landmarks of ecological or cultural significance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Influence of Tradition on Contemporary Nigerian Life</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Traditional institutions (obas, emirs, chiefs) still play advisory and ceremonial roles in modern governance.</li><li>Traditional dispute resolution methods complement the formal justice system in many communities.</li><li>Festivals such as Eyo, Argungu, Durbar and New Yam festivals continue to draw national and international attention.</li><li>Traditional values of respect for elders, communal living and hospitality persist alongside modern lifestyles.</li><li>Indigenous naming ceremonies, marriage rites and burial customs remain widely practised.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Balancing Tradition and Modernity</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Modern Nigeria faces the challenge of preserving valuable heritage while embracing beneficial change — for example, modern law now sits alongside customary law, and technology is increasingly used to document and preserve oral traditions and cultural practices before they are lost.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to give specific Nigerian examples of tangible, intangible and natural heritage, not just definitions.</div>",
