    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Human rights</span> are basic rights and freedoms that belong to every person simply because they are human. In Nigeria, several institutions exist to protect and promote these rights.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Human Rights</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Human rights are fundamental entitlements — such as the right to life, dignity, freedom of expression and fair trial — that belong to every person regardless of nationality, ethnicity, religion, gender or status, and that no government may legitimately take away.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The National Human Rights Commission (NHRC)</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">The NHRC is a statutory body established to promote and protect human rights in Nigeria. It investigates complaints of human rights violations, provides recommendations to government, and raises public awareness about human rights issues.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The Judiciary</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Nigeria's courts play a central role in human rights protection by interpreting the Constitution, hearing cases of rights violations, and providing remedies such as compensation or the enforcement of rights through fundamental rights enforcement procedures.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Non-Governmental Organisations (NGOs)</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Role of NGOs</th><th>Example of activity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Advocacy</td><td>Campaigning for policy change on specific rights issues</td></tr><tr><td>Monitoring</td><td>Documenting and reporting human rights abuses</td></tr><tr><td>Legal aid</td><td>Providing free or low-cost legal support to victims</td></tr><tr><td>Public education</td><td>Raising awareness of citizens' rights and how to claim them</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Challenges to Human Rights Protection in Nigeria</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Slow judicial processes and case backlogs.</li><li>Limited enforcement capacity of the NHRC.</li><li>Poor awareness of rights among citizens, especially in rural areas.</li><li>Impunity for some human rights violators, particularly security personnel.</li><li>Underfunding of human rights institutions.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the three-part structure: NHRC (statutory investigator), judiciary (legal enforcement), NGOs (advocacy and support) — each plays a distinct role.</div>",
