    "module": "Civic Development",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Civic competence</span> is the combination of knowledge, skills and values that equips a citizen to participate effectively and responsibly in public life and contribute to nation building.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Components of Civic Competence</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Knowledge</td><td>Understanding of government structures, rights, duties, laws and current affairs</td></tr><tr><td>Skills</td><td>Critical thinking, communication, problem-solving, collaboration and conflict resolution</td></tr><tr><td>Values</td><td>Honesty, respect, tolerance, responsibility and commitment to the common good</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Why Civic Competence Matters</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Enables citizens to make informed decisions, including how they vote.</li><li>Equips citizens to hold leaders accountable effectively.</li><li>Supports constructive participation in community and national development.</li><li>Reduces susceptibility to manipulation, misinformation and extremism.</li><li>Builds a more cohesive, functional and prosperous society.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Developing Civic Competence</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Through formal education in civic and citizenship studies.</li><li>Through participation in civic activities — debates, clubs, volunteering.</li><li>Through exposure to diverse perspectives and respectful dialogue.</li><li>Through mentorship from civically engaged role models.</li><li>Through access to reliable information and media literacy training.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Civic Competence and Nation Building</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">A critical mass of civically competent citizens is essential for sustainable nation building: such citizens participate in elections thoughtfully, demand accountability, contribute constructively to public discourse, and work collaboratively to solve shared problems, rather than depending solely on government action.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the three components of civic competence — knowledge, skills and values — as this framework is commonly tested.</div>",
