    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Strong exam performance in Citizenship and Heritage Studies depends not only on knowledge of the content, but also on understanding exam structure, command words, and effective answering strategies.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Understanding Command Words</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Command word</th><th>What it requires</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Define/State</td><td>Give a precise, concise meaning</td></tr><tr><td>Explain</td><td>Give reasons or make something clear with detail</td></tr><tr><td>Discuss</td><td>Consider a topic from multiple angles, with supporting points</td></tr><tr><td>Compare/Contrast</td><td>Identify similarities and/or differences between two things</td></tr><tr><td>Enumerate/List</td><td>Give a set of points, usually briefly, often without detailed explanation</td></tr><tr><td>Justify</td><td>Give reasons or evidence to support a position</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Common Focus Areas</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Definitions of key concepts — citizenship, national identity, human rights, good governance.</li><li>Nigerian institutions and their functions — NHRC, judiciary, INEC, NCMM.</li><li>Nigeria's international memberships and roles — UN, AU, ECOWAS.</li><li>Specific examples and case studies — ECOMOG, SDGs, #NotTooYoungToRun.</li><li>Causes, effects and solutions to national challenges — corruption, insecurity, poor leadership.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Strategies for Answering Theory Questions</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Read the question carefully and identify exactly what is being asked before writing.</li><li>Plan a brief outline for essay-type questions before writing the full answer.</li><li>Use clear paragraphs, each covering one distinct point.</li><li>Support general points with specific Nigerian examples wherever possible.</li><li>Manage time carefully, allocating time according to the marks available for each question.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Common Mistakes to Avoid</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Writing everything known about a topic instead of answering the specific question asked.</li><li>Giving vague answers without concrete examples.</li><li>Ignoring the specific command word (e.g. writing a description when asked to 'compare').</li><li>Poor time management leading to rushed or unanswered questions.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Practise past WAEC/NECO questions on Citizenship and Heritage Studies to become familiar with how topics are typically phrased and structured.</div>",
